const List<HeritageSection> heritageSections = [
  HeritageSection(
    title: 'The Ancient Origins of Hindu Temples',
    paragraphs: [
      'The history of Hindu temples in India stretches back over two millennia, forming one of the most remarkable chapters in the story of human civilization. These sacred structures did not appear suddenly; they evolved gradually from simple open-air altars and fire pits of the Vedic period into the towering, intricately carved stone edifices that define the Indian landscape today. Understanding this evolution requires us to trace the spiritual, architectural, and cultural currents that shaped the subcontinent over thousands of years.',
      'During the Vedic period (1500–500 BCE), worship was primarily conducted in the open, around sacrificial fire altars called yagna-kundas. The concept of a permanent dwelling for a deity had not yet crystallized. The Rigveda speaks of celestial abodes of the gods but makes no mention of earthly temples. Rituals were performed by Brahmin priests in temporary structures, and the divine was invoked through mantras, hymns, and offerings of ghee, soma, and grain into the sacred fire. The cosmos itself was the temple — the sky its dome, the earth its floor, and the cardinal directions its walls.',
      'The transition from Vedic fire altars to permanent shrines began during the later Vedic and early post-Vedic periods. Archaeological evidence from sites like Vidisha, Sanchi, and Besnagar in Madhya Pradesh suggests that the earliest structural shrines date to the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE. These were modest structures — flat-roofed, single-celled chambers housing a deity image or symbol, surrounded by a low railing or wall. The Heliodorus Pillar at Besnagar, erected around 110 BCE by a Greek ambassador who was a devotee of Vasudeva (Krishna), stands as one of the earliest physical testimonies to organized Vaishnavite worship at a specific sacred site.',
      'The Gupta period (4th–6th century CE) is often called the Golden Age of Indian temple architecture. It was during this era that the Hindu temple assumed its classical form. The Dashavatara Temple at Deogarh in Uttar Pradesh, dating to approximately 500 CE, is among the finest surviving examples. Built of sandstone, it features a square sanctum (garbhagriha) raised on a high plinth, with exquisitely carved panels depicting scenes from the Ramayana and the Mahabharata. The temple at Bhitargaon in Kanpur, one of the oldest surviving brick temples in India, demonstrates the sophistication of Gupta-era construction techniques. These early temples established foundational principles that would govern temple construction for the next fifteen centuries: the centrality of the garbhagriha (womb chamber), the importance of the shikhara (tower) above it, and the mandapa (pillared hall) before it.',
      'By the 7th century CE, regional styles had begun to diverge significantly. The Chalukyas of Badami created magnificent rock-cut cave temples at Badami and structural temples at Aihole and Pattadakal, experimenting freely with both Dravidian and Nagara forms. Aihole, in present-day Karnataka, is sometimes called the "cradle of Indian temple architecture" because of the extraordinary diversity of temple styles found there — over 125 temples spanning multiple centuries and architectural experiments. The Durga Temple at Aihole, with its apsidal (semi-circular) plan reminiscent of Buddhist chaitya halls, demonstrates how Hindu temple builders absorbed and transformed earlier architectural traditions.',
    ],
  ),
  HeritageSection(
    title: 'The Pallava Legacy: Dawn of Dravidian Architecture',
    paragraphs: [
      'The Pallava dynasty of Kanchipuram (6th–9th century CE) occupies a pivotal place in the history of South Indian temple architecture. Under rulers like Mahendravarman I and Narasimhavarman I (Mamalla), the Pallavas pioneered the transition from rock-cut cave temples to freestanding structural temples, laying the foundation for the Dravidian architectural tradition that would reach its zenith under the Cholas and Vijayanagara emperors.',
      'Mahendravarman I, himself an accomplished poet, musician, and painter, commissioned some of the earliest rock-cut cave temples in Tamil Nadu. The Mandagapattu cave temple, which he personally dedicated to the Hindu trinity of Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva, bears an inscription in which the king proudly declares that it was created without the use of bricks, timber, metal, or mortar — a testament to the skill of Pallava stone-carvers. The cave temples at Trichy (Tiruchirapalli), Dalavanur, and Mahendravadi followed, each more refined than the last, their walls adorned with elegant pilasters and sculpted panels.',
      'The coastal town of Mahabalipuram (Mamallapuram), named after Narasimhavarman I, became the Pallava dynasty\'s greatest artistic showpiece. Here, sculptors carved an astonishing array of monuments directly from the granite outcrops along the shore. The Pancha Rathas (Five Chariots) — Dharmaraja, Bhima, Arjuna, Draupadi, and Nakula-Sahadeva — are monolithic temples carved from single boulders, each showcasing a different architectural form. They served as experimental prototypes, exploring the possibilities of temple design before these forms were replicated in structural stone.',
      'The Shore Temple at Mahabalipuram, built by Narasimhavarman II (Rajasimha) in the early 8th century, represents the culmination of Pallava architectural achievement. This structural temple — as opposed to the rock-cut monuments — stands on the edge of the Bay of Bengal, its twin towers dedicated to Shiva catching the first and last rays of the sun. The Shore Temple introduced several features that became standard in Dravidian architecture: the pyramidal vimana (tower over the sanctum) composed of diminishing tiers (talas), the octagonal shikhara at the apex, and the surrounding prakara (boundary wall) with rows of Nandi bulls. UNESCO recognized its universal significance by inscribing it as a World Heritage Site in 1984.',
      'The Kailasanatha Temple in Kanchipuram, also built by Rajasimha around 700 CE, is perhaps the most architecturally significant Pallava temple. Its sandstone walls are covered with some of the finest sculptural relief in all of Indian art — scenes of Shiva in his various aspects, dancing, meditating, destroying the demon Andhaka, and standing with Parvati. The temple\'s plan, with its main shrine, subsidiary shrines arranged along the prakara wall, and a detached front mandapa, established the template that Chola temple builders would later develop to monumental proportions.',
    ],
