  HeritageSection(
    title: 'The Vijayanagara Empire: A Renaissance of Temple Arts',
    paragraphs: [
      'The Vijayanagara Empire (1336–1646 CE), with its capital at Hampi in present-day Karnataka, presided over a remarkable renaissance of Hindu temple arts and architecture. Founded by Harihara I and Bukka Raya I with the blessings of the sage Vidyaranya, the empire was established in part as a bulwark against the southward expansion of the Delhi Sultanate. For over three centuries, Vijayanagara rulers lavished patronage on temple construction, producing a distinctive architectural style that combined elements from Chola, Hoysala, Chalukya, and Pandya traditions.',
      'The Vijayanagara style is characterized by its monumental scale, exuberant decoration, and innovative structural features. The empire\'s architects introduced several hallmarks that defined late medieval South Indian temple architecture: the kalyana mandapa (marriage hall), a large, ornate pillared hall used for ritual weddings of the temple deities; the utsava mandapa (festival hall), used during temple processions; the amman shrine, a separate temple for the goddess within the main temple complex; and, most distinctively, the raya gopura — a towering gateway tower of unprecedented height that became the primary visual statement of the temple. Under Vijayanagara patronage, gopurams grew from modest gateways into the soaring, sculpture-encrusted towers that now define the skylines of cities like Madurai, Srirangam, and Tiruvannamalai.',
      'The Virupaksha Temple at Hampi, the principal shrine of the Vijayanagara empire, exemplifies this architectural exuberance. Dedicated to Lord Shiva as Virupaksha (a form of Shiva with distorted or fearsome eyes), the temple has been in continuous worship since at least the 7th century, long before the Vijayanagara Empire adopted it as their royal temple. The Vijayanagara rulers expanded it dramatically, adding massive gopurams, elaborately carved pillared halls, and a ceremonial street lined with colonnaded pavilions. The temple\'s 50-metre-high main gopuram, its intricate ceiling paintings depicting scenes from the Ramayana and Mahabharata, and the inverted shadow of the gopuram visible through a pinhole on the inner wall of the main mandapa are among its most celebrated features.',
      'Perhaps the most impressive legacy of Vijayanagara temple architecture is the Thousand Pillar Hall tradition. The Meenakshi Amman Temple in Madurai contains a hall with 985 carved pillars, each unique, including the famous musical pillars that produce distinct musical notes when struck. The Ranganathaswamy Temple at Srirangam — the largest functioning Hindu temple complex in the world, spread across 156 acres with seven concentric prakaras — reached its final form largely under Vijayanagara and Nayak patronage. These vast temple-cities, with their concentric walls, towering gopurams, pillared bazaars, and sacred tanks, represented the most ambitious attempt in Indian history to create a complete sacred landscape — an earthly echo of the celestial city of the gods.',
      'The fall of Vijayanagara in 1565 at the Battle of Talikota was a catastrophe for South Indian temple culture, but it did not end it. The Nayak dynasties that succeeded the Vijayanagara rulers in various regions — the Madurai Nayaks, the Thanjavur Nayaks, the Keladi Nayaks — continued the tradition of temple patronage, often on an even more lavish scale. Thirumalai Nayak of Madurai (r. 1623–1659) added the spectacular Pudu Mandapa to the Meenakshi Temple, with its life-sized sculptural portraits of the Nayak kings and their queens. The Nayak period also saw the flowering of the Theppam (temple float festival) tradition, where deity images are floated on elaborately decorated rafts in temple tanks — a practice that continues to this day in temples across Tamil Nadu.',
    ],
